/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*-------------------------------*\
  envelope02_c3c4c5_branch_stop -- where a pressure-marched saturation trace
  ends, and what it says when it does.

  HISTORY, kept because it is why the op carries its guards.  This case was
  named envelope02_root_jump.  Both saturation solvers bracket T in 200-700 K
  and are warm-started from the previous pressure step; with a wide bracket,
  Newton can converge to a DIFFERENT root and report success.  On this
  C3/C4/C5 feed the first version of the envelope traced the bubble branch
  243 -> 332 K to 16 bar and then reported 268 K at 19 bar -- a bubble
  temperature FALLING as pressure rises, which is thermodynamically
  impossible -- and the cricondentherm detector read the resulting spike on
  the dew branch as a maximum and published 371.492 K.  The op now rejects a
  bubble step that lowers T and a dew step many times its neighbours, and
  reports either as a ROOT JUMP.

  WHAT CHANGED (DEV.md C235, 2026-11-07).  The jump WAS an artefact of the
  liquid reference: with a real vapour, K = gamma Psat / (phi_V P) counted the
  vapour's non-ideality on one side only, and the solver's second root was
  that error's.  With the reference carrying phi_sat and Poynting, a search of
  twelve neighbours of this case (six compositions x PengRobinson and SRK, to
  60 bar) found NO root jump.

  WHAT CHANGED AGAIN (DEV.md C239, 2026-11-08): THE BRANCHES NOW CROSS Tc.
  Until then every branch stopped, without a jump, just below PROPANE's
  critical temperature (369.83 K; last converged points 366-369 K): above Tc
  the liquid reference dropped phi_sat and Poynting (factor exactly 1), a STEP
  in K at T = Tc that a pressure-marched solve cannot cross.  The factor is
  now CONTINUOUS in T: above 0.99 Tc it is HELD at its value at 0.99 Tc
  (announced once, "reference factor of propane held"), so there is no step.
  What the case shows now:

    * the bubble branch passes propane's Tc (near 22 bar) and reaches the top
      of the requested range, 40 bar, in 40 points -- it no longer stops;
    * the dew branch passes Tc (near 16 bar) and runs to 35 bar; at 36 bar
      (401.5 K at the last kept point) the saturation solve converges to a
      DIFFERENT ROOT, the op's guard discards that point and reports a ROOT
      JUMP, and no cricondentherm is claimed.  That stop is a limit of the
      warm-started, bracketed solve far above Tc, NOT of the mixture;
    * the held factor is an extrapolation for a hypothetical liquid: it
      removes the step, it does not make the hypothetical liquid real.

  The case keeps its name (a branch still stops, now at 36 bar on the dew
  side by the root-jump guard); its counts are the golden rows.

  NOT VALIDATED: no number here is checked against a measured envelope.  This
  is a STRUCTURAL witness for what a trace does across a component's Tc.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

application   choupoProps;
description  "P-T envelope of a C3/C4/C5 feed marched through propane's Tc: the reference factor is held continuous above 0.99 Tc, so both branches cross Tc; the dew branch is ended later by the root-jump guard, and no cricondentherm is claimed";
verbosity     3;
